00:28:41.780 --> 00:28:43.640
As a knitted vest with buttons.
一件带扣子的针织背心。

613
00:28:43.660 --> 00:28:45.380
Then they added arMs.
然后他们加上了袖子。

614
00:28:46.300 --> 00:28:50.640
And it soon became sort of like a staple of the British Army's attire.
它很快就成为了英军服装的一种标配。

615
00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:53.540
And it was this garment.
就是这件衣服。

616
00:28:53.640 --> 00:28:58.240
It could keep them warm in battle, but it was also easily removed because they used to
它能在战斗中为他们保暖，但也很容易脱下来，因为他们以前

617
00:28:58.240 --> 00:29:00.500
have like pullover sweaters and stuff.
穿的是套头毛衣之类的东西。

618
00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:00.840
Right.
没错。

619
00:29:01.020 --> 00:29:02.800
But you don't have time for that.
但你可没时间弄那个。

620
00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:03.820
You're in battle.
你正在打仗呢。

621
00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:04.260
Yeah.
对啊。

622
00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:06.620
You know, you got to get that off quick if you're hot.
你知道的，要是觉得热，你得赶紧把它脱掉。

623
00:29:06.780 --> 00:29:06.960
Right.
没错。

624
00:29:07.380 --> 00:29:12.340
Also, unlike a traditional military jacket, it didn't restrict their movement.
此外，与传统的军服外套不同，它不会限制他们的活动。

625
00:29:12.340 --> 00:29:14.680
It was like really easy to move their arMs.
活动手臂非常轻松。

626
00:29:14.940 --> 00:29:16.500
I would relate, by the way.
顺便说一句，我很能感同身受。

627
00:29:16.700 --> 00:29:16.980
Yes.
是的。

628
00:29:17.060 --> 00:29:17.980
He would love this.
他会很喜欢这个。

629
00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:20.580
Because he needs circulation to his hands.
因为他的手需要血液循环。

630
00:29:20.740 --> 00:29:20.940
Yes.
是的。

631
00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:23.160
Knitted military jacket, basically.
基本上就是针织军服外套。

632
00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:28.220
Transforms into the cardigan and was made famous by this guy.
演变成开衫，并因这个人而名声大噪。

633
00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:33.840
So then by the late 1800s, it kind of transitioned into everyday fashion.
到了19世纪末，它逐渐演变成了日常时尚。

634
00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:38.100
It was mostly worn by gentlemen while hunting or fishing.
它主要由绅士们在打猎或钓鱼时穿着。

635
00:29:38.580 --> 00:29:41.740
So this really originated as a men's garment.
所以这最初确实是一件男士服饰。

636
00:29:41.960 --> 00:29:42.220
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

637
00:29:42.340 --> 00:29:46.780
But then by the early 20th century, it made its way into women's fashion.
但到了20世纪初，它进入了女性时尚界。

638
00:29:47.300 --> 00:29:51.300
This was because knitting became a very popular hobby for women.
这是因为编织成了女性非常流行的爱好。

639
00:29:51.720 --> 00:29:54.980
The cardigan was really an easy thing to knit and customize.
开衫确实是一种很容易编织和定制的东西。

640
00:29:55.280 --> 00:29:56.980
It was post-war.
那是战后时期。

641
00:29:57.340 --> 00:29:58.860
People were trying to save money.
人们都在设法省钱。

642
00:29:59.520 --> 00:30:03.640
The sweater really moved into women's fashion as well.
毛衣也确实开始进入女性时尚。

643
00:30:03.860 --> 00:30:06.940
And then Coco Chanel loved the cardigan.
然后可可·香奈儿非常喜欢开衫。

644
00:30:07.060 --> 00:30:11.800
And she is credited with bringing the cardigan into the forefront of high fashion.
人们认为正是她将开衫带到了高级时装的前沿。

645
00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:17.160
Do you see how the cardigan has moved from the battlefield all the way to Coco Chanel?
你发现开衫是如何从战场一路走到可可·香奈儿手里的吗？

646
00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:17.820
Yeah.
对啊。

647
00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:18.660
Come on.
真是不可思议。

648
00:30:19.140 --> 00:30:28.280
Then in the 1950s, the cardigan gained popularity in the United States because people like Grace Kelly and Marilyn Monroe were frequently photographed wearing a cardigan.
然后在20世纪50年代，开衫在美国大受欢迎，因为像格蕾丝·凯利和玛丽莲·梦露这样的人经常被拍到穿着开衫。

649
00:30:28.280 --> 00:30:31.120
For women, cardigans were considered stylish and preppy.
对女性来说，开衫被认为时尚且具有学院风。

650
00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:33.800
For men, they were still going strong.
对男性来说，它依然长盛不衰。

651
00:30:33.800 --> 00:30:40.120
But they were sort of like if you were an intellectual or an academic, like Albert Einstein wore a lot of cardigans.
但它们有点像是，如果你是一个知识分子或学者，比如阿尔伯特·爱因斯坦就经常穿开衫。

652
00:30:40.580 --> 00:30:43.260
So if you were a smarty pants, you might be in a cardigan now.
所以如果你是个聪明鬼，你现在可能就会穿一件开衫。

653
00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:43.660
Right.
没错。

654
00:30:43.660 --> 00:30:50.720
And then, of course, in the 1980s, with grunge fashion, you had Kurt Cobain rocking the slouchy cardigans.
然后，当然了，在20世纪80年代的垃圾摇滚时尚中，库尔特·柯本穿着那种宽松懒散的开衫。

655
00:30:50.920 --> 00:30:54.200
So the cardigan is kind of just an amazing item of clothing.
所以开衫简直就是一件神奇的衣物。

656
00:30:54.540 --> 00:30:55.880
It has evolved over time.
它随着时间不断演变。

657
00:30:55.880 --> 00:30:57.500
It has taken so many forMs.
它有过多得数不清的款式。

658
00:30:57.740 --> 00:31:03.080
But in all of its forms, it is functional, practical.
但无论是哪种款式，它都很实用、很讲究功能性。

659
00:31:03.080 --> 00:31:09.780
It can be styled up or down, and it's a really great way to add warmth to an outfit or to remove if you get too hot.
它可以搭配出正式或休闲的风格，而且真的是一种为穿搭保暖或者在觉得太热时脱掉的好方法。

660
00:31:09.900 --> 00:31:19.940
And that brings me to Pam, who wears her cardigan to face a sort of different kind of artillery, the word vomit that is Michael Scott.
这就让我想到了帕姆，她穿着开衫去面对一种不同类型的炮火——也就是迈克尔·斯科特那滔滔不绝的废话。

661
00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:27.440
I feel like that's a special battle that she went to every day in her cardigan.
我觉得这是她穿着开衫每天都要经历的一场特殊战斗。

662
00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:27.740
Yep.
对。

663
00:31:27.740 --> 00:31:29.800
I wore so many cardigans on The Office.
我在《办公室》里穿了超级多开衫。

664
00:31:30.120 --> 00:31:36.640
I still regret that I didn't, like, capitalize on that and put out a line of cardigans.
我到现在还后悔当时没有借此大捞一笔，推出一个开衫系列呢。

665
00:31:37.160 --> 00:31:38.680
You still can.
你现在还可以啊。

666
00:31:39.080 --> 00:31:39.740
I know.
我知道。

667
00:31:40.160 --> 00:31:41.220
Never say never.
永远别说绝不。

668
00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:47.100
I could totally see Pam's sweaters brought to you by Jenna Fisher at some department store.
我完全能想象得到，某家百货公司里摆着由詹娜·费舍为您呈现的帕姆同款毛衣。

669
00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:48.220
I know.
我知道。

670
00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:49.960
How do people do that?
大家是怎么做到这一点的？

671
00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:52.000
How do you get a perfume?
你怎么才能推出一款香水？

672
00:31:52.400 --> 00:31:52.660
I don't know.
我不知道。

673
00:31:52.660 --> 00:31:53.580
How do you get a wine?
你怎么才能推出一款葡萄酒？

674
00:31:53.860 --> 00:31:54.020
I don't know.
我不知道。

675
00:31:54.020 --> 00:31:55.460
How do you get a cardigan?
你怎么才能推出一款开衫？

676
00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:56.540
Like, I don't understand.
就是，我搞不懂。

677
00:31:56.920 --> 00:31:57.580
I don't know.
我不知道。

678
00:31:57.580 --> 00:32:00.280
Lady, remember when we very first started the podcast?
蕾迪，还记得我们刚开始做播客的时候吗？

679
00:32:00.280 --> 00:32:01.740
I'm talking 2019.
我说的是2019年。

680
00:32:01.740 --> 00:32:03.280
You're going to bring up the rosé.
你又要提起那款桃红葡萄酒了。

681
00:32:03.380 --> 00:32:06.340
I said I wanted an office lady's rosé.
我说过我想要一款“办公室女郎”桃红葡萄酒。

682
00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:08.120
You about killed me with that.
你当时差点把我给整崩溃。

683
00:32:08.340 --> 00:32:09.960
I started doing research.
我开始做调研。

684
00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:12.580
Lady, I talked to a winery about it.
蕾迪，我为此跟一家酒庄谈过。

685
00:32:12.620 --> 00:32:13.080
I know.
我知道。

686
00:32:13.660 --> 00:32:15.500
And it just was like, it was so hard.
然后就觉得，这太难了。

687
00:32:15.500 --> 00:32:16.280
It's a whole other job.
这完全是另一份工作。

688
00:32:16.380 --> 00:32:17.820
It's like another job.
就像又多了一份工作一样。

689
00:32:18.140 --> 00:32:18.500
Yes.
是的。

690
00:32:18.640 --> 00:32:19.780
And we're like, we already have a job.
然后我们就会想，我们本来就有一份工作了。

691
00:32:21.320 --> 00:32:26.480
But, like, how do people have the bandwidth for all these, like, variations of themselves?
但是，人们怎么会有精力去应付所有这些不同的自我衍生形态呢？

692
00:32:26.480 --> 00:32:27.360
I don't know.
我不知道。

693
00:32:27.540 --> 00:32:27.800
I don't know.
我不知道。

694
00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:31.240
But I love that you immediately were like, you're going to talk about the rosé.
但我喜欢你一听就立刻猜到“你又要说桃红葡萄酒了”的反应。

695
00:32:31.620 --> 00:32:33.960
I mean, that was like a year of my life.
我是说，那几乎占了我一年的生活。

696
00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:34.380
Anyway.
总之。

697
00:32:34.780 --> 00:32:36.640
I mean, Bryan Cranston.
我的意思是，布莱恩·克兰斯顿。

698
00:32:36.960 --> 00:32:38.300
They have their whole mezcal.
人家就有自己的梅斯卡尔酒品牌。

699
00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:38.940
Mezcal.
梅斯卡尔酒。

700
00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:39.760
I know.
我知道。

701
00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:42.460
Where's the office lady's rosé?
办公室女职员的桃红葡萄酒呢？

702
00:32:43.100 --> 00:32:43.940
Where is it?
在哪儿呢？

703
00:32:44.020 --> 00:32:45.180
Where's Pam's cardigans?
帕姆的开衫呢？

704
00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:45.840
I don't know.
我不知道。

705
00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:48.580
Jenna Fisher's cardigans.
詹娜·费舍的开衫。

706
00:32:48.680 --> 00:32:49.240
I don't know.
我不知道。

707
00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:50.260
I don't know.
我不知道。

708
00:32:50.260 --> 00:32:51.820
Angela Martin's cat beds.
安吉拉·马丁的猫窝。

709
00:32:52.100 --> 00:32:53.780
Where are these things?
这些东西都去哪儿了？

710
00:32:54.680 --> 00:32:55.920
Who does this?
谁干的这种事？

711
00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:57.440
How do you do the side hustle?
副业是怎么搞的？

712
00:32:57.580 --> 00:32:58.440
Someone write us.
有人给我们写信了。

713
00:32:59.240 --> 00:32:59.620
Ah.
啊。

714
00:33:00.380 --> 00:33:03.420
So, anyway, I wore a lot of cardigans on the office.
所以，总之，我在《办公室》里穿了很多开衫。

715
00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:08.920
And we've discussed before how the costume department made each of us a closet of clothes.
我们之前讨论过服装部门是怎么为我们每个人准备一个衣橱的。

716
00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:09.460
Yeah.
对。

717
00:33:09.760 --> 00:33:12.760
And mine had pencil skirts, button-down shirts, and cardigans.
我的衣橱里有铅笔裙、衬衫和开衫。

718
00:33:12.920 --> 00:33:15.520
And they would just mix and match them for various episodes.
他们会在不同的剧集里把这些衣服混搭着穿。

719
00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:22.640
And it was really important to the reality of our characters that we repeat clothings so
这对于我们角色的真实感非常重要，那就是我们要重复穿衣服，所以

720
00:33:22.640 --> 00:33:23.980
you see things repeat.
你会看到衣服重复出现。

721
00:33:24.300 --> 00:33:31.840
And it's funny because I found a ton of articles of people breaking down Pam's cardigans according
很有意思的是，我发现了很多文章，人们根据颜色以及它们与剧集的关系来分析帕姆的开衫

722
00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:35.900
to their colors and how they relate to the episodes.
。

723
00:33:36.100 --> 00:33:39.660
And I just want everyone to know that was intentional.
我只想让大家知道，那是刻意为之的。

724
00:33:39.960 --> 00:33:41.200
You are right on.
你们分析得完全正确。

725
00:33:41.200 --> 00:33:46.660
And my color palette was mostly pastels, like shades of pink or mint green or light blue.
我的色调主要是柔和的颜色，比如粉红色、薄荷绿或浅蓝色。

726
00:33:46.900 --> 00:33:49.240
But I did also wear some more muted colors.
但我也确实穿过一些更柔和、低调的颜色。

727
00:33:49.420 --> 00:33:50.220
There were some grays.
有一些灰色。

728
00:33:50.340 --> 00:33:51.040
There were some beiges.
有一些米色。

729
00:33:51.580 --> 00:33:55.320
And there was purpose to what colors were chosen each week.
每周选择什么颜色都是有目的的。

730
00:33:55.480 --> 00:33:59.480
Like, for example, I'm wearing a beige cardigan in the episode The Secret.
比如，我在《秘密》那一集里穿着一件米色开衫。

731
00:33:59.960 --> 00:34:06.340
And that is the episode where Jim tells Pam that he 100% does not have a crush on her anymore.
那一集讲的是吉姆告诉帕姆他百分之百不再对她有好感了。

732
00:34:06.340 --> 00:34:14.680
And so we wanted me to be, like, muted, you know, because my heart is being muted.
所以我们希望我显得比较低调、黯淡，你知道的，因为我的心正在变得黯淡。

733
00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:15.180
Yeah.
对。

734
00:34:15.520 --> 00:34:18.140
And then also there were times when things were scripted.
另外，有时候剧情是有剧本安排的。

735
00:34:18.360 --> 00:34:22.560
Like in the merger, Pam says, oh, my mom knitted this for me.
比如在公司合并那一集里，帕姆说，哦，这是我妈妈给我织的。

736
00:34:23.000 --> 00:34:23.420
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

737
00:34:23.940 --> 00:34:29.580
There's a lot of chatter online about whether or not that was truly a hand-knit cardigan.
网上有很多讨论，关于那到底是不是一件手工织的开衫。

738
00:34:29.680 --> 00:34:34.280
I want everyone to know when we broke down that episode, I revealed that it was not.
我想让大家知道，当我们复盘那一集时，我透露了其实并不是。

739
00:34:34.280 --> 00:34:37.780
It was store-bought, but there were modifications made.
那是买来的成衣，但做过一些修改。

740
00:34:37.960 --> 00:34:39.800
We added that big chunky button.
我们加上了那颗大号粗呢纽扣。

741
00:34:40.060 --> 00:34:42.680
We tried to add things to make it look hand-knit.
我们试图添加一些元素来让它看起来像是手工织的。

742
00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:46.060
But I mean, it's like we got that script one week.
但我的意思是，我们差不多一周前才拿到那一集的剧本。

743
00:34:46.200 --> 00:34:48.520
Who's, like, fast-knitting this sweater for me?
谁能在这么短时间内给我快织出一件毛衣？

744
00:34:48.560 --> 00:34:52.280
I guess they could have found a hand-knit sweater, but we're going for something specific.
我想他们或许能找到一件手工织的毛衣，但我们需要特定款式的。

745
00:34:52.580 --> 00:34:58.840
Also, though, in a time crunch, it's just harder to find those very specific handmade things.
而且话说回来，在时间紧迫的情况下，要找到那些非常独特的手工制品本来就很难。

746
00:34:59.060 --> 00:35:01.400
And you need duplicates of everything.
并且每样东西都需要备用件。

747
00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:07.040
Every outfit we had two of, in case you got coffee spilled on it or who knows what.
我们的每套衣服都有两份，以防衣服上洒了咖啡或者发生别的什么情况。

748
00:35:07.200 --> 00:35:07.880
I don't know.
我不知道。

749
00:35:08.140 --> 00:35:11.760
But every outfit had a duplicate outfit.
但每套衣服都有备用的同款服装。

750
00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:16.940
So to find two identical hand-knit sweaters in five days, that was a challenge.
所以要在五天内找到两件一模一样的手工毛衣，那可真是个挑战。

751
00:35:17.160 --> 00:35:17.900
Yeah, exactly.
对，完全正确。

752
00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:22.240
The color I wore the least that I only wore for holiday episodes was red.
我穿得最少、只在节日特别集里穿的颜色是红色。

753
00:35:22.240 --> 00:35:29.480
And my favorite cardigan is that vintage holiday cardigan that I wear.
我最喜欢的开衫就是我穿的那件复古节日开衫。

754
00:35:29.840 --> 00:35:31.400
It's so great.
真的很棒。

755
00:35:31.500 --> 00:35:32.660
I'm still bummed I don't have it.
我到现在还是很遗憾没有留着它。

756
00:35:32.780 --> 00:35:36.340
We have scoured the internet to see if there's another one out there.
我们在网上到处搜过，看看还能不能找到另一件。

757
00:35:36.480 --> 00:35:37.340
They don't make it anymore.
他们不生产了。

758
00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:37.900
Yes.
是的。

759
00:35:38.260 --> 00:35:43.180
But everyone, if you pay attention to Pam's arc, whatever she's going through emotionally,
但是大家注意潘的成长历程，无论她当时在经历怎样的情绪波澜，

760
00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:46.260
and you note her cardigan, there is often a correlation.
只要你留意她的开衫，就会发现它们之间往往存在某种联系。

761
00:35:46.420 --> 00:35:47.660
And that was intentional.
而且那是刻意安排的。

